{"type":"assistant","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The problem was in how minutes were derived. The code used `seconds // 60`, which is total minutes since zero, not the minute field within an hour. At exactly 3600 seconds you got `1h 60m 0s` instead of `1h 0m 0s`.\n\nMinutes are now taken from the remainder after hours: `(seconds % 3600) // 60`, with seconds unchanged as `seconds % 60`. That matches the usual HMS breakdown and keeps multi-hour values correct (e.g. 3661 → `1h 1m 1s`).\n\nThe stale BUG comment was removed; `old_format_hms` is unchanged and still delegates to `format_hms`. Both visible tests pass."}]},"session_id":"4fb9fd3a-c9f6-4125-8e1e-d96d86c1e3fc"}
{"type":"result","subtype":"success","duration_ms":67309,"duration_api_ms":67309,"is_error":false,"result":"Reviewing `timefmt.py` and how it's used in the repo.\n\n\nFixing minute calculation: minutes used total minutes (`seconds // 60`) instead of minutes within the hour.\n\n\nThe problem was in how minutes were derived. The code used `seconds // 60`, which is total minutes since zero, not the minute field within an hour. At exactly 3600 seconds you got `1h 60m 0s` instead of `1h 0m 0s`.\n\nMinutes are now taken from the remainder after hours: `(seconds % 3600) // 60`, with seconds unchanged as `seconds % 60`. That matches the usual HMS breakdown and keeps multi-hour values correct (e.g. 3661 → `1h 1m 1s`).\n\nThe stale BUG comment was removed; `old_format_hms` is unchanged and still delegates to `format_hms`. Both visible tests pass.","session_id":"4fb9fd3a-c9f6-4125-8e1e-d96d86c1e3fc","request_id":"2ccc9c86-8d25-41bf-9a80-2a0b1487ebcf","usage":{"inputTokens":11672,"outputTokens":1448,"cacheReadTokens":78368,"cacheWriteTokens":0}}
